          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe as initial management for reflux before any investigation?",
          "answer": "Guidance on the initial management of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) suggests an empiric approach. For adults experiencing typical symptoms such as heartburn and regurgitation, and without alarm features, an eight-week trial of acid suppression using a proton pump inhibitor is recommended. This approach allows for symptom assessment before any investigative procedures like endoscopy. It is noted that the timing of medication intake is critical; medication should be taken before meals to maximize effectiveness, as proton pump inhibitors act on activated pumps in the stomach (source: Gastroenterology society clinical guideline).\n\nThis management strategy emphasizes that many patients may benefit from this trial without further investigation initially, as the response to acid suppression provides valuable information for ongoing care.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
